# Atlas Design Controls -- Artefact Enforcement

Every Class B code change must be fully traceable in the Atlas MCP
server through the artefact chain:

```
UN (user need) -> DI (requirement) -> DO (implementation spec)
                                  -> Risk (if safety-relevant)
                                  -> VT (verification)
                                  -> VAL (if clinical outcome)
```

All steps below are **blocking** -- do not write code until the gap
is resolved. Every step uses the Atlas MCP server
`user-atlas-design-controls`. Never write YAML files. See
`atlas-design-controls-first.mdc`.

Product ID: `PROD-LEO-CLINICAL-DECISION-SUPPORT-20260515-118182E4`.

---

## Scope -- Class C is out of scope of Mizaic's manufacturing policy

LEO Core AI is a clinical decision support device. Every clinical
fact it produces is a proposal for a person to judge, and that
judgement happens outside LEO. Per IEC 62304 clause 4.3, mandatory
operator intervention is a non-software risk control external to
the SOFTWARE SYSTEM, which keeps every software item in this
product at Class B or below. See the SAD `architecture_overview`.

**Mizaic's manufacturing policy does not permit Class C software in
this product.** As of 2026-04-20 there are zero Class C artefacts in
Atlas for `PROD-LEO-CLINICAL-DECISION-SUPPORT-20260515-118182E4`, and
this rule keeps it that way.

If a proposed change cannot be made Class B -- for example a feature
that would publish, export, or autonomously act on AI output --
**stop**:

1. Do **not** classify the change as Class C and proceed.
2. Escalate to the Clinical Safety Officer and the QA/RA lead before
   any code is written.
3. Either redesign so the change stays Class B, or raise an
   intended-purpose / safety-case change request that formally
   extends the device scope.

The `safety_class` field on every Atlas artefact in this product is
therefore restricted to `A` or `B`. The legacy `C` value is kept in
the Atlas enum for forward defence (so a genuinely autonomous
feature can be added later without schema work) but **must not be
used without prior escalation**.

---

## Workflow before writing any code

### Step 0 -- Classify the change (proportionality gate)

| Class       | Description                                      | Examples                                                  | Obligations                                              |
|-------------|--------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| Cosmetic    | No logic, no safety impact                       | CSS tweaks, copy changes, variable renames                | Look up nearest DI in Atlas to confirm no overlap; no new artefacts |
| Class A     | Non-safety-critical (IEC 62304 Class A)          | Internal tooling, dev-only utilities, test helpers        | Look up DIs and risks in Atlas; create only if a genuinely new capability |
| Class B     | Safety-relevant (IEC 62304 Class B)              | Any clinical pipeline, API route, UI feature for clinical staff | **Full Atlas chain required**: UN, DI, DO, Risk, VT |
| Class C     | Out of scope -- not manufactured under Mizaic's policy for this product | Anything that publishes, exports, or autonomously acts on AI output | **Stop and escalate** to Clinical Safety Officer + QA/RA lead before writing code. Redesign to stay Class B, or raise an intended-purpose change request. |

If unsure, treat as Class B.

### Step 1 -- Find or create the User Need

```
search_design_controls(query="<feature in plain English>", dc_type="user_need")
```

- If a relevant UN exists -> note its dcId (e.g. `UN-A2F0AE7F`) and
  proceed.
- If none exists -> **stop** and call:

```
create_user_need(
  product_id="PROD-LEO-CLINICAL-DECISION-SUPPORT-20260515-118182E4",
  title="<short title>",
  description="As a <stakeholder>, I need ...",
  rationale="<why this need exists>",
  stakeholder_types=["Clinician" | "Quality Engineer" | "Developer"
                     | "IT Admin" | "Quality Manager" | "External Auditor"
                     | "Knowledge Base Author"],
  acceptance_criteria=["<plain-English criterion>", ...],
  priority="MUST" | "SHOULD" | "COULD" | "WONT",
  safety_class="A" | "B",   # "C" requires prior escalation; see Scope section above
)
```

Stakeholder enum values are strict; ask for help if your stakeholder
does not fit.

### Step 2 -- Find or create the Design Input

```
search_design_controls(query="<implementation surface>", dc_type="design_input")
```

If none exists for the functionality:

```
create_design_input(
  product_id=...,
  title="<short title>",
  description="The platform SHALL ... <SHALL statement>",
  rationale="<why>",
  input_type="functional" | "safety" | "performance" | "interface" | "usability",
  acceptance_criteria=["<measurable criterion>", ...],
  safety_class="A" | "B",   # "C" requires prior escalation; see Scope section above
  verification_method="test",
)
```

Then link to the parent UN:

```
link_design_controls(
  source_ids=["UN-XXXXXXXX"],
  target_ids=["DI-YYYYYYYY"],
  relationship="HAS_DESIGN_INPUT",
)
```

### Step 3 -- Find or create the Design Output

```
get_design_control(dc_type="design_input", dc_id="DI-YYYYYYYY")
# inspect the linked nodes; confirm a DO with IMPLEMENTS exists
```

If none exists:

```
create_design_output(
  product_id=...,
  title="<short title> (implementation)",
  description="<what this module does, key files, key functions>",
  acceptance_criteria=["<measurable criterion>", ...],
  output_type="software" | "documentation" | "test" | "configuration",
  source_reference="path/to/file1.py; path/to/file2.ts",
  software_item="<short logical name>",
  safety_class="A" | "B",   # "C" requires prior escalation; see Scope section above
)
link_design_controls(
  source_ids=["DO-ZZZZZZZZ"],
  target_ids=["DI-YYYYYYYY"],
  relationship="IMPLEMENTS",
)
```

### Step 4 -- Identify or create a Risk (if safety-relevant)

If the change introduces or modifies a safety-relevant behaviour:

```
search_design_controls(query="<hazard description>", dc_type="risk")
```

If no covering risk exists:

```
create_risk(
  product_id=...,
  title="<one-sentence>",
  hazard_description="<what could go wrong>",
  hazardous_situation="<sequence of events from hazard to harm>",
  harm_description="<what harm to patient / user / org>",
  harm_severity="negligible" | "minor" | "serious" | "critical" | "catastrophic",
  hazard_category="clinical_safety" | "data_protection" | "usability"
                  | "security_breach" | "availability" | "data_integrity"
                  | "software_defect" | "misuse" | "performance_degradation"
                  | "integration_failure" | "infrastructure_failure" | "use_error",
  initial_severity=1..5, initial_probability=1..5, initial_detectability=1..5,
  initial_risk_score=<S*P*D>,
  mitigation_plan_description="<what the controls do, with implemented_by/verified_by>",
  mitigation_control_type="design_feature" | "protective_measure"
                           | "procedural_control" | "information_for_safety",
  mitigation_effectiveness="effective" | "partially_effective" | "pending",
  monitoring_plan="<how this risk is monitored post-deployment>",
  monitoring_metrics=["<metric>", ...],
  review_frequency="Quarterly" | "Per release" | "Annually",
  residual_severity=1..5, residual_probability=1..5, residual_detectability=1..5,
  residual_risk_score=<S*P*D>,
  residual_acceptability="acceptable" | "conditionally_acceptable" | "unacceptable",
  residual_justification="<why the residual is acceptable>",
  identified_by="<source>", identified_date="YYYY-MM-DD",
  status="Identified" | "Analyzed" | "Control Implemented"
         | "Residual Accepted" | "Closed" | "Inactive" | "Obsolete",
  safety_class="A" | "B",   # "C" requires prior escalation; see Scope section above
)
link_design_controls(
  source_ids=["DI-YYYYYYYY"],
  target_ids=["R-WWWWWWWW"],
  relationship="MITIGATES",
)
```

If an existing risk's mitigation is affected -> use `update_risk` to
refresh `mitigation_plan_description`, `residual_*`, `monitoring`,
and (if needed) `mitigation_effectiveness`.

Class B DIs MUST have at least one MITIGATES relationship from the
DI to a Risk. Class A DIs may have none. The risk's `safety_class`
MUST be greater than or equal to the linking DI's `safety_class` --
a Class B DI cannot be mitigated only by a Class A risk.

### Step 5 -- Find or create the Verification Test

```
get_design_control(dc_type="design_input", dc_id="DI-YYYYYYYY")
# confirm a VT with VERIFIES exists
```

If none exists:

```
create_verification_test(
  product_id=...,
  title="<short title> -- acceptance verification",
  description="<what is verified and why it matters>",
  test_type="unit" | "integration" | "system" | "inspection",
  verification_method="test" | "inspection" | "analysis" | "demonstration",
  acceptance_criteria=["<measurable>", ...],   # 1-for-1 with the DI's ACs
  protocol_steps=[
    "Step 1: ...",
    "Step 2: ...",
    "Capture pass/fail evidence and record via record_verification_run.",
  ],
  safety_class="A" | "B",   # "C" requires prior escalation; see Scope section above
)
link_design_controls(
  source_ids=["VT-VVVVVVVV"], target_ids=["DI-YYYYYYYY"],
  relationship="VERIFIES",
)
link_design_controls(
  source_ids=["VT-VVVVVVVV"], target_ids=["DO-ZZZZZZZZ"],
  relationship="TESTS",
)
```

### Step 6 -- Run the test and record the run

After implementing or modifying tests:

```bash
pytest <test_file_path> -v
# or
cd services/ui && npx jest --testPathPatterns "<pattern>" --no-coverage
```

Record the run in Atlas (this is what makes a VT "passed"):

```
record_verification_run(
  vt_id="<VT UUID>",          # from get_design_control(...).id
  result="pass" | "fail",
  executed_by="<who or ci>",
  notes="<pass count, fail count, any context>",
  environment="ci" | "local" | "staging",
)
```

Use the **VT UUID** from `get_design_control(...).id`, NOT the
`dcId`. Several Atlas tools require the UUID, not the dcId.

### Step 7 -- Create the Validation Test (clinical-outcome features)

For features that change a clinical outcome visible to users
(coding decisions, safety thresholds, tier classifications, alert
escalation rules):

```
search_design_controls(query="<clinical outcome>", dc_type="validation_test")
```

If none covers it:

```
create_validation_test(...)
link_design_controls(
  source_ids=["VAL-UUUUUUUU"], target_ids=["DI-YYYYYYYY"],
  relationship="VALIDATES",
)
record_validation_run(...)
```

